@*
    The one screen every new user sees, and the only one with room to say where the
    instructions live and what the app promises before it touches anything. Once a folder has
    been scanned this never shows again — the rail's Plan tab takes over.
*@
<div class="firstrun">
    <div class="firstrun-scan">
        <span class="eyeb">Nothing scanned yet</span>
        <h1>Point SnapZap at a folder.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
            It reads everything underneath, works out which photos are copies of each other,
            and hands you a clean library to export. All of it on this machine.
        </p>
        <ScanForm />
        <div class="hint" style="margin-top:10px">Enter scans. A leading ~ expands to your home folder.</div>
        <hr class="hr" />
        <div class="formats">
            Reads JPEG · PNG · WebP · GIF · TIFF · BMP. HEIC/HEIF, AVIF and camera RAW are
            counted and reported, never touched — so a folder of iPhone photos never looks
            like an empty scan.
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top:20px"><button class="btn btn-ghost" @onclick="() => OnOpenHelp.InvokeAsync()">How SnapZap works</button></p>
    </div>
    <div class="firstrun-promises">
        <span class="eyeb">Two promises</span>
        <div class="promise">
            <h4>Your originals stay put</h4>
            <p>
                Scanning only reads. Files move in exactly two cases, both of which you have to
                ask for: an export in Move mode, or ticking "also recycle what I didn't pick".
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="promise">
            <h4>Nothing is ever hard-deleted</h4>
            <p>
                Delete means the Recycle Bin. Every batch lands in History with a Restore next
                to it, for as long as the bin holds it.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="firstrun-shown-once">SHOWN ONCE. NOT REPEATED LATER.</div>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnOpenHelp { get; set; }
}
